@page "/tech"

@* How the game is actually built. Every claim here is something the repository does — the opcodes are
   the ones ArkadeCovenants emits, the flows are the ones that run, and where something is enforced by
   the server rather than by a covenant it says so. TechPageTests keeps the opcode table honest. *@

<PageTitle>How it works · Arkade Heroes</PageTitle>

<div class="page-head">
    <h2>How it works</h2>
    <span class="hud">covenants, assets, and why you don't have to trust us</span>
    <span class="spacer"></span>
    <a class="btn ghost" href="/">← back to the arena</a>
</div>

<p class="muted codex-intro">
    Most games ask you to trust a database. This one is built so you mostly don't have to: your heroes
    are Bitcoin assets you hold, the rules that move them are enforced by script rather than by our good
    behaviour, and every outcome we publish can be re-derived on your own machine.
</p>

@* ---- ASSETS ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- *@
<div class="page-head" style="margin-top:2rem"><h3 style="margin:0">A hero is an asset, not a row</h3>
    <span class="hud">arkade assets on bitcoin</span></div>
<div class="grid cols">
    <div class="card">
        <h4 style="margin:0 0 .5rem">Yours, in your wallet</h4>
        <p class="muted tech-p">
            Each hero is an Arkade asset held at your own address. We never custody it. If this server
            disappeared tomorrow the asset would still be yours, and the strand that defines the creature
            is committed in its genesis — so anybody can rebuild the same hero from it.
        </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
        <h4 style="margin:0 0 .5rem">The strand is the record</h4>
        <p class="muted tech-p">
            We do not store a hero's stats, look or lineage anywhere. They are <em>derived</em> from the
            32 committed values every time. There is no field for us to edit, which is a stronger promise
            than one we would have to keep.
        </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
        <h4 style="margin:0 0 .5rem">Gear and XP too</h4>
        <p class="muted tech-p">
            Equipment and earned experience are assets in their own right, not counters on an account.
            That is what lets them be staked, traded and — in a death match — genuinely lost.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

@* ---- ARKADE SCRIPT --------------------------------------------------------------------- *@
<div class="page-head" style="margin-top:2.2rem"><h3 style="margin:0">Arkade Script</h3>
    <span class="hud">the rules, written as spending conditions</span></div>
<p class="muted cx-note">
    Arkade Script lets a spending condition look at the transaction that is trying to spend it. That is
    the whole trick: instead of asking a server to promise it will do the right thing, the coin itself
    refuses to move unless the transaction has the right <b>shape</b>.
</p>

<div class="card">
    <div class="hud">The simplest one we use — pay exactly this address, exactly this amount</div>
    <pre class="tech-code"><code>DUP INSPECTOUTPUTSCRIPTPUBKEY 1 EQUALVERIFY &lt;witness program&gt; EQUALVERIFY
INSPECTOUTPUTVALUE &lt;amount&gt; EQUAL</code></pre>
    <p class="muted tech-p" style="margin-top:.6rem">
        The witness supplies an output index; the script insists that output is a P2TR paying a specific
        key, and that its value is exactly the amount baked into the script. Nobody can redirect it,
        including us.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="card" style="margin-top:1rem;overflow-x:auto">
    <div class="hud" style="margin-bottom:.6rem">The introspection opcodes this game leans on</div>
    <table class="codex-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Op</th><th>Name</th><th>What the covenant learns</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            @foreach (var op in Opcodes)
            {
                <tr>
                    <td class="mono">@op.Code</td>
                    <td class="mono">@op.Name</td>
                    <td class="muted">@op.Does</td>
                </tr>
            }
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="codex-foot" style="margin-top:.7rem">
        The asset ones are what make a game possible rather than just a payment. A covenant that can count
        the assets at an output, find a group by asset id, and sum a group across inputs and outputs can
        express "this hero must be destroyed", "the child must land here", "exactly one asset, to that
        player" — as a spending rule.
    </p>
</div>

@* ---- STRUCTURAL ------------------------------------------------------------------------ *@
<div class="page-head" style="margin-top:2.2rem"><h3 style="margin:0">Structural enforcement</h3>
    <span class="hud">shape, not signatures</span></div>
<p class="muted cx-note">
    An earlier version of these covenants worked by having the server sign off on each outcome. That is
    only as good as the server. The ones running now check the transaction's structure instead, so the
    rule holds even against the operator.
</p>
<div class="grid cols">
    @foreach (var c in Covenants)
    {
        <div class="card">
            <div class="hud">@c.Name</div>
            <h4 style="margin:.25rem 0 .5rem">@c.Headline</h4>
            <p class="muted tech-p">@c.Detail</p>
        </div>
    }
</div>

@* ---- SWAPS ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- *@
<div class="page-head" style="margin-top:2.2rem"><h3 style="margin:0">Non-interactive swaps</h3>
    <span class="hud">nobody has to be online</span></div>
<div class="card">
    <p class="muted tech-p" style="max-width:76ch">
        Listing a hero or an item does not park it with us. The seller deposits the asset into a covenant
        address built from the offer's own terms, and then walks away. <b>Anyone</b> can fulfil it later
        by building one transaction that takes the asset and pays the seller the ask — the covenant will
        not permit any other shape, so the swap is atomic: either both legs happen or neither does.
    </p>
    <div class="tech-flow">
        <span class="tech-step">seller deposits the asset</span>
        <span class="tech-arrow">→</span>
        <span class="tech-step">offer rests at its own address</span>
        <span class="tech-arrow">→</span>
        <span class="tech-step">any buyer pays the ask in one tx</span>
        <span class="tech-arrow">→</span>
        <span class="tech-step out">asset and payment settle together</span>
    </div>
    <p class="codex-foot" style="max-width:76ch">
        No escrow agent, no counterparty online, no trusted matching. If nobody buys, the seller reclaims
        it after the offer expires — again without asking us. The marketplace fee is a leg of the same
        covenant, so it cannot be skipped and cannot be inflated.
    </p>
</div>

@* ---- RANDOMNESS ------------------------------------------------------------------------ *@
<div class="page-head" style="margin-top:2.2rem"><h3 style="margin:0">Randomness you can check</h3>
    <span class="hud">commit, contribute, reveal</span></div>
<div class="card">
    <div class="tech-flow">
        <span class="tech-step">we publish SHA256(seed)</span>
        <span class="tech-arrow">→</span>
        <span class="tech-step">you add your own nonce</span>
        <span class="tech-arrow">→</span>
        <span class="tech-step">the action resolves</span>
        <span class="tech-arrow">→</span>
        <span class="tech-step out">we reveal the seed</span>
    </div>
    <p class="muted tech-p" style="max-width:76ch">
        We commit to a secret seed <em>before</em> knowing what you will do, and you mix in a value we
        cannot predict. Afterwards the seed is revealed, and the commitment proves it is the same one —
        so we could not have picked a seed that suited us, and you could not have picked a nonce that
        suited you. The entropy is then <span class="mono">SHA256(seed ‖ context)</span>, with every part
        length-prefixed so two different contexts can never collide into the same roll.
    </p>
    <p class="codex-foot" style="max-width:76ch">
        Everything downstream is a pure function of that entropy: a bred child, a fusion, an absorbed
        trait, a bracket. Nothing rolls behind a curtain.
    </p>
</div>

@* ---- VERIFIABLE OUTCOMES ---------------------------------------------------------------- *@
<div class="page-head" style="margin-top:2.2rem"><h3 style="margin:0">Fights you can re-run</h3>
    <span class="hud">don't trust the result — recompute it</span></div>
<div class="card">
    <p class="muted tech-p" style="max-width:76ch">
        Combat is deterministic. Given the two heroes, their gear and the revealed entropy, the fight has
        exactly one outcome, and your own client replays it blow by blow to confirm the one we reported.
        The same holds for a bred child, a fusion, an absorb, a gauntlet, a squad match and a tournament
        bracket — each has its own verifier.
    </p>
    <p class="muted tech-p" style="max-width:76ch">
        Rules change over time, so every published outcome carries a stamp of the rules version it was
        resolved under, and the exact rule set for any version can be fetched and checked. An old replay
        stays verifiable under the rules it actually ran on rather than silently failing against today's.
        A server that served rules other than the ones it stamped is caught by your client's own
        recompute — not taken on its word.
    </p>
</div>

@* ---- MUTATION -------------------------------------------------------------------------- *@
<div class="page-head" style="margin-top:2.2rem"><h3 style="margin:0">Mutation, without a dice roll we control</h3>
    <span class="hud">deterministic, domain-separated</span></div>
<div class="card">
    <p class="muted tech-p" style="max-width:76ch">
        A breeding does not "roll" anything at runtime. The parents and the entropy are hashed into a
        fixed pool of bytes, and each trait reads its own slice of that pool to decide what is inherited,
        whether a hidden trait surfaces, and whether a mutation fires. Same inputs, same child, forever —
        which is exactly what makes it checkable.
    </p>
    <p class="muted tech-p" style="max-width:76ch">
        Each operation hashes with its own domain tag, so the pool that decides a <b>breeding</b> can
        never be reused as the pool that decides a <b>fusion</b>, an <b>absorb</b>, or whether a hero is
        born <b>sterile</b>. Without that separation, one roll could be replayed to predict another.
    </p>
</div>

@* ---- LIMITS ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- *@
<div class="page-head" style="margin-top:2.2rem"><h3 style="margin:0">What is not covenant-enforced</h3>
    <span class="hud">the honest part</span></div>
<div class="card">
    <p class="muted tech-p" style="max-width:76ch">
        Not everything is. Matchmaking, the ladder, the daily loop and the season pot are ordinary server
        logic — the covenants secure <em>custody and settlement</em>, not every rule of play. What that
        buys you is the part that matters most: nobody can take a hero off you, mint one that was never
        bred, spend a stake to the wrong place, or strand your escrow. Where you must trust the operator,
        it is about scheduling and bookkeeping rather than ownership.
    </p>
    <p class="codex-foot" style="max-width:76ch">
        And if the operator vanishes mid-flow, the escrows have timelocked reclaim paths you can take
        yourself — that is what the reclaim page is for.
    </p>
</div>

<p class="codex-note muted" style="margin-top:2rem">
    Sats in this game are real bitcoin. That is the point, and it is also the risk — read the terms.
</p>

@code {
    record Op(string Code, string Name, string Does);

    // The opcodes ArkadeCovenants actually emits. Pinned by TechPageTests against the constants there.
    static readonly Op[] Opcodes =
    [
        new("0xc9", "INSPECTINPUTVALUE", "What an input being spent was worth."),
        new("0xcf", "INSPECTOUTPUTVALUE", "What an output is paying."),
        new("0xd1", "INSPECTOUTPUTSCRIPTPUBKEY", "Who an output pays — the check behind every 'must go here'."),
        new("0xe5", "INSPECTNUMASSETGROUPS", "How many distinct assets this transaction touches at all."),
        new("0xe8", "FINDASSETGROUPBYASSETID", "Whether a specific asset is present, and where."),
        new("0xeb", "INSPECTASSETGROUP", "An asset group's output index and amount."),
        new("0xec", "INSPECTASSETGROUPSUM", "An asset's total across inputs, outputs, or both — how a burn is proven."),
        new("0xed", "INSPECTOUTASSETCOUNT", "How many assets sit at one output — how 'exactly one' is enforced."),
    ];

    record Cov(string Name, string Headline, string Detail);

    static readonly Cov[] Covenants =
    [
        new("Breeding", "The child must land in your wallet",
            "Both parents are escrowed, and the covenant only releases when the transaction actually "
            + "creates the child asset at the output paying you. The server cannot keep the child, "
            + "swap it, or quietly mint a second one."),
        new("Fusion", "Two in, one out — and the second is really gone",
            "The sacrificed hero must be destroyed in the same transaction that forges the result. "
            + "There is no path that produces the new hero while leaving the old one alive."),
        new("Death match", "The loser is burned, the winner is paid",
            "Both heroes and both stakes go into one joint escrow. Settlement is only valid if the "
            + "losing hero's asset is provably burned and the pot lands at the winner — checked by "
            + "summing the asset across the transaction, not by trusting a result we posted."),
        new("Offers", "Take the asset only by paying the ask",
            "The item or hero rests in a covenant that permits exactly two shapes: a fulfilment that "
            + "pays the seller (and the fee) in the same transaction, or a reclaim by the seller after "
            + "expiry. Nothing else spends it."),
        new("Wagers", "The pot can only go to a party",
            "A staked match escrows both stakes. The settle path is bound to the parties' own scripts, "
            + "so the pot cannot be paid to a third address even by the server that arbitrates."),
        new("Reclaim", "You can always get your own stake back",
            "Every escrow has a timelocked path you can take without the server's help, so a crash, a "
            + "disappearance or an abandoned opponent cannot strand your hero or your sats."),
    ];
}
